# LLVM Intrinsic Call-Boundary Repair

schema_version: 1
directive: `INBOX-34 prerequisite correctness repair`
target: `x86_64-linux-gnu`
validation_tier: 3
result: pass

The composed call selector now rejects every unimplemented `llvm.*` intrinsic
before the ordinary scalar SysV direct-call path. This corrects three prior
false-positive report emissions: `compiler_rt.cmp.__cmpti2` retained
`llvm.scmp.i32.i128`, `compiler_rt.cmp.__ucmpti2` retained
`llvm.ucmp.i32.i128`, and `compiler_rt.bitreverse.__bitreversedi2` retained
`llvm.bitreverse.i64`. These names are LLVM IR operations, not native runtime
symbols, so those objects were not valid closure evidence.

The named-inequality acceptance probe also exposed an earlier unresolved
`llvm.fabs.f64` call in the four comparison wrappers targeted by INBOX-34.
The selector now lowers that exact typed intrinsic by moving the f64 payload to
a GPR, clearing only its sign bit, and moving it back to XMM0. Permanent
executable coverage proves exact bits for negative zero, a minimum subnormal,
and quiet/signaling NaN payloads. An unknown `llvm.sqrt.f64` call proves the
new fail-closed boundary.

Fresh report-all moves from the previously accepted but incorrect 443 selected
/ 229 emitting / 214 failing to 443 selected / 226 emitting / 217 failing.
There are no new emissions. Other affected rows only advance to the more
accurate `composed-block-llvm-intrinsic` diagnostic.

```text
summary zllvm-codegen-report-all target=x86_64-linux selected_functions=443 emitted_functions=226 failed_functions=217 local_fail=1
focused tests: unit=926/926 f64_fabs_payload_cases=4/4 unknown_intrinsic_fail_closed=1/1
tier2: pass; steps=24/24 tests=934/934 structural=8/8 assembly/encoding/object inspection=pass integrated_executable=5/5
```

